  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "It happens to the best of us: you're sanding drywall, and suddenly you've got a gouge, or the surface just isn't as smooth as you hoped. Don't sweat it! First, check if you're sanding wet compound. That's a common culprit for unevenness and can make things worse. Make sure your joint compound is completely dry—usually around 24 hours, but it can vary. If you're pressing too hard, ease up; light pressure is key, letting the sandpaper do the work. Using the wrong grit can also cause issues. Start with a medium grit for most areas, then move to a finer grit for a super smooth finish. If you've got low spots or larger imperfections, sanding won't fix them; you'll need to apply more joint compound and build it up in thin layers. Don't try to sand away big problems! If you're consistently getting waves or can't achieve that perfectly flat, paint-ready surface, it might be time to call in someone like Abraham and his team at HH Remodeling LLC. We’ve got the experience to get those walls looking just right."
    }
  ],
